## Priority 1. Systematic vaccination and seasonal respiratory infection prevention

**Why this priority matters:** Respiratory infections are a major driver of winter primary-care workload, emergency attendance, hospital admission and staff absence. Vaccination is one of the most scalable preventive interventions, although effectiveness varies by pathogen, strain, age and season.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1.1 Increase influenza vaccination among older adults, pregnant women, people with long-term conditions and frontline staff** | Policy/organisational/clinical. Reduces infection, severe disease and transmission. Use opportunistic vaccination in primary care, pharmacies, maternity services, outpatient clinics and workplaces. | **Low–medium**. Requires vaccine supply, call-recall, pharmacy and primary-care capacity, mobile/community delivery and culturally appropriate communication. | **Strong for individual protection; moderate for reducing all-cause winter pressure.** Influenza vaccine effectiveness is variable but vaccination remains standard public-health practice (Cochrane evidence; Demicheli et al., 2018). Feasible within 3 years. Barriers include hesitancy, access, misinformation and staff time. | Uptake by risk group, ethnicity, deprivation and occupation; vaccination-related missed-opportunity rate; influenza admissions; staff sickness absence. |
| **1.2 Improve COVID-19 booster uptake in high-risk groups** | Policy/organisational. Reduces severe respiratory disease and hospitalisation. | **Low–medium**. Requires risk-stratified call-recall, pharmacy and community access, and integration with influenza campaigns. | **Strong for reduction of severe COVID-19; moderate for overall winter-pressure reduction.** Protection wanes and varies by variant and previous immunity (UKHSA, 2023; Andrews et al., 2022). Within 3 years. | Booster uptake; admissions and deaths among eligible groups; inequalities in uptake; missed opportunities. |
| **1.3 Improve pneumococcal vaccination among eligible adults** | Clinical/organisational. Reduces invasive pneumococcal disease and some pneumococcal pneumonia. | **Low–medium**. Use GP records, pharmacy delivery and opportunistic vaccination during COPD, diabetes and frailty reviews. | **Strong for invasive pneumococcal disease; less certain for all-cause pneumonia and A&E reduction.** Evidence from conjugate vaccine trials supports disease-specific protection (Bonten et al., 2015). Within 3 years. | Uptake in eligible adults; invasive pneumococcal disease; vaccine-preventable admissions. |
| **1.4 Introduce targeted infection-prevention support for care homes** | Organisational/policy. Rapid testing, outbreak protocols, vaccination, staff exclusion when infectious and early clinical review can reduce transmission and delayed escalation. | **Medium**. Requires care-home IPC support, clear escalation routes and access to testing and vaccination. | **Moderate for outbreak control; limited direct evidence that bundled programmes reduce NHS winter admissions.** Highly context-sensitive. Within 3 years. | Outbreaks; vaccination uptake; respiratory admissions from care homes; time from deterioration to clinical review. |

**Classification:** 1.1–1.3 are **strong-evidence interventions**, although their effect on total winter demand is indirect. Care-home bundles are **promising and context-dependent**.

