## Priority 2. Proactive management of COPD, asthma and other respiratory long-term conditions

**Why this priority matters:** Exacerbations of COPD and asthma contribute substantially to winter admissions. Many patients also have cardiovascular disease, diabetes, frailty, anxiety, depression or multimorbidity.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **2.1 Deliver personalised COPD/asthma self-management plans with inhaler technique review** | Clinical/organisational. Early recognition of deterioration and correct treatment may reduce severe exacerbations and inappropriate emergency use. | **Low–medium**. Requires trained clinicians, structured templates, interpreter access and pharmacy involvement. | **Moderate.** Written action plans and supported self-management have evidence of benefit, although effects vary by programme (Lenferink et al., 2017). Within 3 years. | Action-plan coverage; inhaler-technique competency; rescue steroid/antibiotic use; exacerbations; ED attendances. |
| **2.2 Establish rapid-access respiratory exacerbation pathways** | Organisational. Same-day or next-day assessment by respiratory nurses/clinicians can treat exacerbations earlier and divert suitable patients from A&E. | **Medium.** Requires extended-hours workforce, diagnostics, oxygen assessment and direct admission/ambulatory pathways. | **Moderate; UK service models are more supportive than definitive.** Feasible in 3–5 years, but workforce-dependent. | Time from referral to assessment; admissions; 30-day readmissions; patient-reported access; safety-netting completion. |
| **2.3 Offer pulmonary rehabilitation after COPD exacerbation and to appropriate stable patients** | Clinical/organisational. Improves exercise capacity, symptoms and self-management; may reduce readmission in selected patients. | **Medium.** Requires physiotherapists, community venues, transport support and home/digital alternatives. | **Strong for functional outcomes and quality of life; moderate for admission reduction.** Pulmonary rehabilitation after exacerbation improves recovery, but timing and patient selection matter (Puhan et al., 2016). Within 3–5 years. | Referral and completion rates; exercise capacity; quality of life; COPD admissions and readmissions; deprivation gap. |
| **2.4 Provide smoking cessation and tobacco-dependence treatment integrated into respiratory care** | Clinical/policy. Reduces future exacerbations and improves cardiovascular and respiratory health. | **Low–medium**. Requires trained advisers, pharmacotherapy, opt-out referral and culturally tailored support. | **Strong for smoking cessation and long-term health; effect on immediate winter pressure is slower.** Within 3 years for service delivery; 5–10 years for population impact. | Quit attempts and abstinence at 4 and 12 weeks; smoking prevalence; COPD exacerbations; inequalities. |

**Classification:** Self-management, pulmonary rehabilitation and smoking cessation are **strong or moderate evidence**. Rapid-access exacerbation pathways are **promising but more dependent on local service configuration**.

