## Priority 3. Hospital-at-home, virtual wards and urgent community response

**Why this priority matters:** These interventions can disrupt the conventional pathway of ambulance–ED–acute bed by assessing and treating selected patients at home.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **3.1 Implement hospital-at-home for carefully selected acute medical patients** | Organisational/policy. Provides hospital-level assessment, treatment, monitoring and escalation at home. | **High**. Requires 24/7 clinical response, diagnostics, medicines, oxygen, equipment, digital and non-digital monitoring, and clear exclusion criteria. | **Strong-to-moderate.** Cochrane evidence suggests hospital-at-home can produce similar outcomes for selected patients, with potential reductions in costs and institutional care, but generalisability is variable (Shepperd et al., 2016). Within 3–5 years. | Patients treated at home; conversion to hospital; mortality; readmission; patient experience; cost per episode; carer burden. |
| **3.2 Standardise urgent community response within two hours for people at risk of admission** | Organisational. Rapid multidisciplinary response addresses acute deterioration, mobility, care needs and medicines in the home. | **Medium–high**. Requires integrated community nursing, therapy, social care, GPs, rapid equipment and shared records. | **Moderate; evidence is stronger for admission avoidance concepts than for uniform two-hour models.** Within 3 years where workforce exists. | Response time; admission avoidance; repeat calls; safeguarding incidents; patient function and experience. |
| **3.3 Use virtual wards only where they provide active clinical treatment, not solely remote observation** | Organisational/policy. Structured monitoring plus proactive clinical intervention allows earlier detection and treatment. | **Medium–high**. Requires clinical staffing, validated pathways, connectivity, device support and analogue alternatives. | **Mixed/promising.** Evidence for remote monitoring alone is inconsistent; benefits depend on selection, response protocols and workforce (NHS England, 2023; Noah et al., 2021). Within 3–5 years. | Admission rate; escalation time; false alerts; patient-reported burden; digital exclusion; staff workload. |
| **3.4 Provide home oxygen, intravenous therapy and diagnostics through community pathways when clinically appropriate** | Clinical/organisational. Enables treatment at home and avoids admission. | **High and context-sensitive**. Requires pharmacy, infection control, diagnostics, trained staff and emergency escalation. | **Promising/case-based for some conditions; not suitable for all patients.** Within 3–5 years. | Hospital days avoided; adverse events; treatment completion; unplanned escalation; patient/carer experience. |

**Classification:** Hospital-at-home has the strongest evidence. Virtual wards and community IV treatment are **promising but context-sensitive** and should not be expanded solely on activity targets.

