## Priority 4. Same-day emergency care and ambulatory emergency pathways

**Why this priority matters:** Same-day emergency care can reduce avoidable overnight admission and improve timeliness, but it requires reliable diagnostics, senior decision-making and downstream community support.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **4.1 Create consultant-led same-day emergency care for suitable medical and surgical presentations** | Organisational. Rapid assessment, diagnostics, treatment and discharge without overnight admission. | **Medium–high**. Requires senior clinicians, diagnostics, pharmacy, therapy, transport and follow-up. | **Moderate.** Observational evidence supports reduced admissions for selected pathways, but confounding and selection effects are substantial. Within 3 years. | Same-day discharge rate; conversion to admission; 72-hour reattendance; mortality; waiting time; patient experience. |
| **4.2 Introduce direct-access pathways from ambulance, primary care and NHS 111 to same-day services** | Organisational/policy. Avoids unnecessary ED registration and duplication. | **Medium**. Requires agreed referral criteria, shared records and clinical accountability. | **Promising; implementation evidence exceeds comparative effectiveness evidence.** Within 3 years. | Direct referrals; avoided ED attendances; safety incidents; referral rejection; time to treatment. |
| **4.3 Provide ambulatory pathways for low-risk pulmonary embolism, cellulitis, heart failure and selected infections** | Clinical/organisational. Risk stratification and outpatient treatment reduce admission. | **Medium**. Requires diagnostics, senior review and reliable follow-up. | **Moderate-to-strong for selected conditions**, particularly low-risk pulmonary embolism, but not transferable to all patients (Yoo et al., 2020). Within 3 years. | Proportion managed ambulantly; adverse events; readmissions; length of stay; patient-reported outcomes. |
| **4.4 Couple same-day emergency care to rapid community follow-up** | Organisational. Prevents deterioration after early discharge. | **Medium**. Requires named clinical responsibility and timely primary/community review. | **Moderate conceptually; direct evidence varies.** Within 3 years. | Follow-up within 48–72 hours; reattendance; admission within 7 and 30 days; unresolved test results. |

**Classification:** Selected ambulatory pathways have **stronger evidence** than generic same-day emergency care. The latter is **moderate and highly implementation-dependent**.

