## Priority 5. Frailty, falls and comprehensive geriatric assessment

**Why this priority matters:** Frailty and falls are major causes of winter ambulance use, ED attendance, prolonged hospital stays, deconditioning and institutionalisation.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **5.1 Provide multidisciplinary comprehensive geriatric assessment in the ED and acute medical unit** | Organisational/clinical. Identifies medical, functional, cognitive, medication and social needs and creates a coordinated plan. | **Medium–high**. Requires geriatricians, therapists, nurses, pharmacists, social care and rapid diagnostics. | **Strong for improving likelihood of living at home and reducing institutional outcomes; admission effects are less consistent** (Ellis et al., 2017). Within 3 years. | CGA coverage; admission rate; length of stay; discharge destination; functional status; 30-day readmission. |
| **5.2 Establish multidisciplinary falls-response services for recurrent fallers** | Clinical/community. Rapid assessment addresses orthostatic hypotension, medicines, vision, mobility and home hazards. | **Medium**. Requires therapy, falls specialists, pharmacy, primary care and housing/social-care links. | **Moderate.** Multifactorial falls interventions benefit selected populations, but effectiveness varies by setting (Hopewell et al., 2018). Within 3–5 years. | Falls, fractures, ED attendances, medication changes, home-hazard completion. |
| **5.3 Offer hospital-to-home reablement within 48 hours of discharge** | Organisational/community. Restores function and reduces deconditioning and premature care-home placement. | **Medium–high**. Requires therapy, domiciliary care and equipment availability. | **Moderate; outcomes depend on intensity and social-care capacity.** Within 3 years. | Time to first visit; function; care-home placement; readmission; patient/carer outcomes. |
| **5.4 Use frailty identification as a trigger for a documented personalised care and escalation plan** | Clinical. Makes goals, treatment ceilings, preferred place of care and reasonable adjustments visible across services. | **Low–medium**. Requires training, shared records and patient/carer involvement. | **Moderate for person-centredness and care consistency; limited direct evidence for reducing admissions.** Within 3 years. | Care-plan completion; patient concordance; unwanted transfers; complaints; preferred-place-of-care achievement. |

**Classification:** CGA is **strong evidence**. Falls services and personalised escalation plans are **moderate**, with substantial local variation.

