## Priority 6. Medicines optimisation and safer transitions of care

**Why this priority matters:** Medication-related harm, polypharmacy and poor transition communication contribute to winter admissions, particularly among older people and those with multimorbidity.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **6.1 Perform pharmacist-led medication reconciliation within 24–72 hours of admission and at discharge** | Clinical/organisational. Identifies omissions, duplications, interactions and inappropriate medicines. | **Medium**. Requires clinical pharmacists, access to GP/community pharmacy records and protected time. | **Moderate.** Medication reconciliation reduces discrepancies; evidence for preventing harm and admission is less consistent (Mekonnen et al., 2016). Within 3 years. | Reconciliation completed; clinically significant discrepancies; medication-related incidents; readmissions. |
| **6.2 Provide structured medicines support after discharge for high-risk patients** | Clinical/community. Telephone or face-to-face review confirms access, understanding, adherence and adverse effects. | **Low–medium**. Can use community pharmacists, practice pharmacists or trained nurses. | **Moderate/mixed.** Transitional medication interventions show variable effects and should target high-risk groups rather than all patients (Ravn-Nielsen et al., 2018). Within 3 years. | Contact within 7 days; medicine-related ED attendance; adherence; patient understanding. |
| **6.3 Use explicit criteria for reviewing high-risk prescribing in frailty and multimorbidity** | Clinical. Reduces adverse effects from sedatives, anticholinergics, NSAIDs, hypoglycaemics and inappropriate combinations. | **Low–medium**. Requires decision support and shared decision-making. | **Moderate for reducing potentially inappropriate prescribing; direct admission reduction uncertain** (Rankin et al., 2018). Within 3 years. | High-risk medicine prevalence; deprescribing decisions; adverse drug events; patient-reported treatment burden. |
| **6.4 Ensure discharge summaries and outstanding results reach the responsible clinician promptly** | Organisational. Prevents duplication, missed diagnoses and unsafe treatment discontinuity. | **Low–medium**. Requires interoperable systems, minimum datasets and accountability. | **Moderate for communication quality; evidence for clinical outcomes is variable.** Within 3 years. | Summary sent within 24 hours; result ownership; post-discharge medication discrepancies; incidents. |

**Classification:** Reconciliation is **moderate evidence** and highly actionable. Broad medication-review programmes are **mixed** unless targeted to high-risk patients.

