## Priority 7. Safe discharge, intermediate care and admission avoidance

**Why this priority matters:** Delayed discharge reduces bed availability, increases infection and deconditioning risk, and amplifies winter pressure.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **7.1 Begin discharge planning on admission, including estimated discharge date and functional goals** | Organisational. Reduces avoidable inpatient days and enables earlier mobilisation and coordination. | **Low–medium**. Requires multidisciplinary board rounds, patient involvement and reliable social-care communication. | **Moderate; evidence is stronger for process improvement than hard outcomes.** Within 3 years. | Length of stay; medically fit days; discharge before noon; delayed transfers; patient experience. |
| **7.2 Expand early supported discharge and intermediate care for clinically stable patients** | Organisational/community. Transfers rehabilitation and recovery to home or step-down settings. | **Medium–high**. Requires therapy, nursing, domiciliary care, equipment and social-care capacity. | **Moderate-to-strong for selected stroke and rehabilitation populations; variable for general medicine** (Langhorne et al., 2017). Within 3–5 years. | Home discharge; functional independence; readmissions; care-home placement; patient/carer burden. |
| **7.3 Create a seven-day discharge and community response model** | Organisational/policy. Prevents weekend bottlenecks and allows discharge when clinically ready. | **Medium–high**. Requires weekend pharmacy, therapy, transport, social care and primary-care interfaces. | **Moderate; seven-day service availability alone does not guarantee improved outcomes.** Within 3 years. | Weekend discharges; length of stay; readmissions; delayed transfers; staffing costs. |
| **7.4 Use “discharge to assess” with rapid assessment in the person’s usual residence** | Organisational. Avoids prolonged inpatient assessment and reduces hospital-associated decline. | **Medium–high**. Requires home-care, therapy, equipment and social-care responsiveness. | **Promising/moderate; evidence is mainly service evaluations and observational studies.** Within 3 years where community capacity exists. | Time from medically fit to discharge; assessment completed at home; care-home admissions; patient function. |

**Classification:** Early supported discharge has **strong evidence in selected conditions**. General discharge-to-assess models are **promising but capacity-sensitive**.

