## Priority 8. Integrated primary and community care for multimorbidity

**Why this priority matters:** Patients with COPD, heart failure, diabetes, renal disease, frailty and mental ill-health often experience fragmented care and repeated emergency use. Single-disease pathways can increase treatment burden.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **8.1 Risk-stratify people at high risk of winter admission and offer proactive care planning** | Organisational/clinical. Identifies deterioration risk and creates anticipatory plans, including contact routes and escalation preferences. | **Low–medium**. Requires validated local risk models, primary-care capacity and patient review. | **Moderate for identifying risk; mixed for reducing admissions unless linked to an effective intervention** (Lewis et al., 2015; newer evaluations remain heterogeneous). Within 3 years. | High-risk patients with plans; admissions; emergency contacts; equity of coverage. |
| **8.2 Provide multidisciplinary case management for complex multimorbidity, with a named coordinator** | Organisational. Coordinates clinical and social needs and supports self-management. | **Medium–high**. Requires care coordinators, GP/consultant input, social care and voluntary-sector links. | **Mixed.** Case management can improve patient experience and care processes, but admission reduction is inconsistent (Stokes et al., 2015). Within 3–5 years. | Patient-reported coordination; care-plan completion; A&E visits; admissions; staff time per patient. |
| **8.3 Use personalised self-management education incorporating respiratory, cardiovascular and mental-health needs** | Clinical/community. Builds confidence, improves symptom recognition and supports medication, activity and lifestyle choices. | **Low–medium**. Requires health coaching, accessible materials, peer support and interpreter provision. | **Moderate**, with stronger evidence for disease-specific outcomes than for multimorbidity as a whole (Smith et al., 2021). Within 3 years. | Self-efficacy; activation; action-plan use; emergency use; quality of life. |
| **8.4 Link people to welfare, housing, fuel-poverty and social-support interventions** | Policy/community. Addresses cold homes, food insecurity, isolation and inability to obtain medicines or care. | **Medium**, with cross-sector coordination. | **Promising and undervalued.** Health effects are plausible and social determinants are important, but direct NHS admission evidence is limited. Within 3–5 years. | Fuel-poverty referrals; housing improvements; missed appointments; winter admissions by deprivation; wellbeing. |

**Classification:** Care planning and self-management are **moderate**. Broad case management and social prescribing are **mixed for admission reduction**, though potentially important for equity and wellbeing.

