## Priority 9. Digital access, remote monitoring and clinical information interoperability

**Why this priority matters:** Digital tools can support early diagnosis, remote review and information sharing, but can worsen inequalities if digital access is assumed rather than supported.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **9.1 Use remote monitoring for selected high-risk respiratory or heart-failure patients with explicit response protocols** | Clinical/organisational. Identifies deterioration and prompts treatment before emergency presentation. | **Medium–high**. Requires validated measures, devices, staff reviewing alerts and rapid treatment access. | **Mixed.** Remote monitoring can improve disease-specific outcomes in selected groups, but generic monitoring has uncertain effects on admissions and workload (Inglis et al., 2015; Noah et al., 2021). Within 3–5 years. | Alert response time; admission rate; false-alert burden; adherence; digital exclusion; patient experience. |
| **9.2 Implement shared care records and real-time access to medicines, diagnoses, care plans and results** | Policy/organisational. Reduces duplication and unsafe decisions across primary, community, ambulance and hospital care. | **Medium–high**. Requires interoperability, information governance, data quality and staff training. | **Moderate for process and safety outcomes; direct winter-pressure evidence is limited.** Within 3–5 years. | Record access; duplicate tests; medication discrepancies; information-related incidents; clinician time. |
| **9.3 Offer digital-first services with a supported non-digital alternative** | Policy/organisational. Improves convenience and timeliness without excluding people unable to use digital channels. | **Low–medium**. Requires telephone, face-to-face and interpreter routes. | **Moderate for access and convenience; effect on overall demand is uncertain.** Within 3 years. | Access by deprivation, disability, age and ethnicity; abandoned contacts; time to appointment; satisfaction. |
| **9.4 Use automated population-health searches to identify missed vaccination, monitoring or follow-up** | Organisational. Converts routine data into proactive prevention. | **Low–medium** if records are interoperable. | **Promising; effectiveness depends on data quality and staff capacity.** Within 3 years. | Recall completion; missed monitoring; vaccination uptake; inequalities; avoidable admissions. |

**Classification:** Interoperability and supported digital access are **pragmatic and moderately supported**. Remote monitoring is **promising but mixed** and should not be treated as a universal solution.

