## Priority 10. Workforce safety, infection prevention and resilient winter operating models

**Why this priority matters:** Staff illness, burnout, violence, moral injury and unsafe workload reduce capacity precisely when demand is highest. Workforce safety is therefore both a staff-welfare priority and a patient-flow priority.

### Actionable interventions

| Intervention | Level and mechanism | Resources and implementation requirements | Evidence, feasibility and barriers | Suggested indicators |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **10.1 Provide seasonal staff vaccination, rapid access to occupational health and evidence-based infection prevention** | Policy/organisational. Reduces staff infection, transmission and sickness absence. | **Low–medium**. Requires occupational-health capacity, vaccination access and ventilation/IPC measures. | **Strong for vaccination and infection prevention principles; direct impact on staffing capacity varies by season.** Within 3 years. | Staff vaccination; sickness absence; healthcare-associated respiratory outbreaks; PPE/IPC compliance. |
| **10.2 Implement reliable staffing escalation and safe workload thresholds** | Organisational/policy. Detects unsafe staffing, reallocates work and triggers mutual aid or service reduction before harm occurs. | **Medium–high**. Requires workforce data, escalation governance and reserve capacity. | **Moderate for association between staffing and outcomes; intervention evidence is less definitive** (Griffiths et al., 2019). Within 3–5 years. | Nurse staffing; missed care; incidents; agency use; sickness; staff experience; mortality/length of stay. |
| **10.3 Establish violence-reduction programmes in ED and urgent-care settings** | Organisational/policy. Environmental design, security, de-escalation training and clear reporting protect staff and patients. | **Medium**. Requires security, training, reporting and redesign of high-risk areas. | **Moderate for reducing incidents in some settings; highly context-dependent.** Within 3 years. | Violence incidents; staff injuries; assaults per attendance; reporting rates; staff perception of safety. |
| **10.4 Use daily system control rooms with agreed escalation triggers for ambulance queues, bed capacity, discharge, infection and staffing** | Organisational/system. Improves shared situational awareness and prioritises actions across organisations. | **Medium**. Requires data, senior decision-makers and authority to act. | **Promising/case-based.** There is limited peer-reviewed evidence that control rooms alone reduce mortality or admissions. Within 3 years. | Ambulance handover time; bed occupancy; medically fit days; discharge delays; escalation actions completed. |

**Classification:** Vaccination and IPC are **stronger evidence**. Control rooms and some workforce models are **important but case-based**, requiring evaluation rather than assuming benefit.

